{"kind": "model", "latency": 90.8, "n_calls": 14, "text": " \nThe low-batch check confirms 128 is competitive early, but 256 is more robust once normal segment noise and the later corpus are included. I\u2019m implementing a conservative controller centered on 256, with a one-time phase-change detector from recent realized steps; after a detected jump it raises the batch to 512 to amortize the larger step workload, while retaining a fallback to 256 if the observed reduction is insufficient.", "calls": ["{\"command\":[\"bash\",\"-lc\",\"apply_patch <<'PATCH'\\n*** Begin Patch\\n*** Delete File: /app/sched.py\\n*** Add File: /app/sched.py\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"Closed-loop batch schedule for the fixed-token run.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+def run(env):\\n+    # The small batches are best on the ramp.  A source change makes the\\n+    # work per segment jump; use the observed jump rather than assuming the\\n+    # dev replica's change location.\\n+    recent = []\\n+    phase2 = False\\n+    batch = 256\\n+\\n+    for i in range(env.n_segments):\\n+        if not phase2 and i >= 5 and len(recent) >= 5:\\n+            baseline = sum(recent[-5:]) / 5.0\\n+            if recent[-1] > baseline * 1.22 and i >= 15:\\n+                phase2 = True\\n+                batch = 512\\n+\\n+        r = env.run_segment(batch)\\n+        steps = float(r[\\\"steps\\\"])\\n+        recent.append(steps)\\n+\\n+        # One very noisy segment should not lock the controller into the\\n+        # larger batch.  Revert only when two observations are both close to\\n+        # the old regime, which also handles an unusually mild phase change.\\n+        if phase2 and len(recent) >= 3 and recent[-1] < recent[-3] * 1.08:\\n+            batch = 256\\n*** End Patch\\nPATCH\\n/app/bin/lab evaluate\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}"], "usage": 1548, "effort": "medium", "t": 1790540111.2566137}
